For an early wizard, it's better to use daggers than spells for
attack. At expert, wizards can throw up to three daggers at once, same
as a valk. Plus, the wizard's first sacrifice gift is Magicbane, which
uses the dagger skill. Poisoned darts can also work if you're
character has high dexterity, but tend to disappear. If you have a
decent (8+) stack of daggers, I'd enchant them rather than magicbane
because thrown daggers score multiple hits.

Oh, and this may feel strange for a former valk, but early wizards
really, really need a pet's help for survival. Tame several pets. If
you can cast "sleep", put monsters to sleep and let the pets do their
stuff. That way, you'll have large cats and dogs before you'll know
it; just make sure you have enough food so that your cat doesn't steal
your lunch.

My recommendation is to ditch the quarterstaff, find a (non-cursed)
dagger, and start using it for melee combat. Then start chucking
daggers at everything that moves. Unless you can find a spellbook of
magic missile, I'd strongly consider wearing metal body armor such as
mithril.

Post magicbane, the game is pretty damn easy. The quest artifact is
the most powerful in the game: it takes care of that little "power
regeneration" issue you noticed. You probably won't need the daggers
because of the magic missile spell. Since magic missile's damage grows
with experience level, a level 30 wizard averages something like 50
damage per hit, doubled if the missile bounces. You will be killing
everything that moves before it can even touch you, without any risk
of draining too much power. I've ascended three wizards, my favourite
role because of the easy endgame. Have fun!

